[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a question about the Lévy reflection theorem / Lévy hierarchy. Let me [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthink carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe Lévy reflection theorem states that for any formula φ, ZFC proves that there[0m[38;2;124;124;124mexists a closed unbounded class of ordinals β such that φ is absolute for V_β. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mMore precisely, for any formula φ of set theory, ZFC proves:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m∀α ∃β > α such that φ is absolute for V_β (i.e., V_β reflects φ).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe standard proof uses the fact that for each subformula, we can find a closed [0m[38;2;124;124;124munbounded class of ordinals where it's absolute, and the intersection of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfinitely many closed unbounded classes is closed unbounded.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow the question is about a sequence {β_n} that is strictly increasing, where [0m
[38;2;124;124;124meach subformula of φ_n is absolute for V_{β_n}.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, I need to understand the setup. There seems to be a sequence of formulas [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mφ_n. The question is whether we can construct a strictly increasing sequence of [0m[38;2;124;124;124mordinals β_n such that each subformula of φ_n is absolute for V_{β_n}.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe Lévy reflection theorem in ZFC: For any formula φ (with parameters), ZFC [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mproves that there is a closed unbounded class of ordinals β such that φ is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mabsolute for V_β. The proof works by showing that for each subformula of φ, the [0m[38;2;124;124;124mclass of β where that subformula is absolute is closed unbounded, and the finite[0m[38;2;124;124;124mintersection of closed unbounded classes is closed unbounded.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor a countable sequence of formulas φ_n, we can indeed construct a strictly [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mincreasing sequence β_n. At each step, given β_{n-1}, we apply the reflection [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtheorem to φ_n (and all its subformulas) to find β_n > β_{n-1} such that all [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msubformulas of φ_n are absolute for V_{β_n}.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe key point: the reflection theorem is provable in ZFC. For each individual [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mformula, ZFC proves the existence of reflecting ordinals. And since we can [0m
[38;2;124;124;124malways pick a larger one (the class is unbounded), we can make the sequence [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstrictly increasing.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut wait — there's a subtlety. The full reflection theorem (for all formulas [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msimultaneously, or for a proper class of formulas) requires a meta-theoretic [0m
[38;2;124;124;124margument or is stated as a theorem scheme. Each instance "for formula φ, there [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mexists a reflecting ordinal" is a theorem of ZFC. But can ZFC prove "for every [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mn, there exists β_n such that..." as a single theorem?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf {φ_n} is a definable sequence of formulas (e.g., an enumeration of all [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mformulas), then the question becomes whether ZFC can prove the existence of the [0m[38;2;124;124;124msequence {β_n}.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me reconsider. The reflection theorem is a theorem scheme in ZFC. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mFor each formula φ, ZFC proves ∃β such that φ reflects at V_β. But ZFC cannot [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mprove the single statement "for all formulas φ, there exists β such that φ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mreflects at V_β" because that would require quantifying over formulas, which is [0m[38;2;124;124;124mnot possible in the first-order language of set theory.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHowever, if we have a specific countable sequence of formulas {φ_n} that is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdefinable (or even just a sequence we're constructing meta-theoretically), then:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mFor each n, ZFC proves ∃β (β > β_{n-1} ∧ φ_n reflects at V_β). So by induction [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(in the meta-theory), for each n, ZFC proves ∃β_1, ..., β_n (strictly [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mincreasing, each reflecting φ_i). But can ZFC prove the existence of the [0m
